        <!-- Closing -->
        <div class="mt-16 bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-8">
          <h2 class="text-2xl font-black text-white mb-4">The Bottom Line</h2>
          <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
            For most households the answer is 1,500 to 2,300 PSI. Vehicles want under 1,500, painted wood and soft siding 1,200-1,500, vinyl siding and fences 1,500-2,000, and wood decks 1,200-1,800. Only concrete, brick and pavers reach into the 2,000-3,000 range, and only when they are genuinely dirty.
          </p>
          <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
            Do not shop on pressure alone. Cleaning units, pressure multiplied by flow, is what compares two machines, and a 2,030 PSI unit moving 1.76 GPM out-cleans a 2,300 PSI unit moving 1.2 GPM by about 30 percent. Check for PWMA or CETA certification too, because an uncertified PSI Max figure may be measured inside the machine with no nozzle fitted.
          </p>
          <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
            Then use less than you bought. Start with the widest nozzle about ten feet back, step down one angle at a time, keep the wand moving, and let a proper cleaner dwell before you reach for more pressure. That sequence removes more dirt, and it is the difference between a deck you cleaned and a deck you have to sand.
          </p>
        </div>


